---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { Image, getImage } from "astro:assets";
import JayStJohnsBay from "../assets/JayStJohnsBay.jpg";
import selflakeportrait from "../assets/selflakeportrait.jpg";

// Absolute portrait URL for the Person structured data (JSON-LD needs a
// fully-qualified image URL, same as Open Graph).
const portrait = await getImage({ src: JayStJohnsBay, width: 800, format: "jpg" });
const portraitUrl = new URL(portrait.src, Astro.site).href;

// JSON-LD: identify Jay as the photographer behind the site. sameAs points at
// his existing off-site presences (the blog and photo gallery).
const personLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Person",
  name: "Jay Brass",
  alternateName: "The Kodak Kid",
  jobTitle: "Photographer",
  description:
    "Nature and landscape photographer, educator, and software engineer — the photographer behind Intelligent Imagery.",
  url: new URL("about/", Astro.site).href,
  image: portraitUrl,
  knowsAbout: [
    "Photography",
    "Nature photography",
    "Landscape photography",
    "Film photography",
  ],
  sameAs: [
    "https://intelligentimagery.blogspot.com/",
    "https://kodakid.wixsite.com/intelligentimageries",
  ],
  worksFor: {
    "@type": "Organization",
    name: "Intelligent Imagery",
    url: "https://intelligentimagery.com/",
  },
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="About Jay Brass — the photographer behind Intelligent Imagery."
  jsonLd={personLd}
>
  <section class="wrapper">
    <article class="bio">
      <h1 class="page-title">About Jay Brass</h1>

      <div class="hero">
        <Image
          class="hero-img"
          src={JayStJohnsBay}
          alt="Jay Brass at St. John's Bay in Newfoundland, wearing a photographer's vest with a camera bag across his chest."
          widths={[480, 720, 1080]}
          sizes="(max-width: 780px) 92vw, 720px"
        />
      </div>

      <p class="lead">
        Engineer by day and sometimes by night, photographer all the time.
      </p>

      <p>
        Jay Brass has loved the outdoors and photography since he was a young boy
        growing up in the foothills of the Adirondacks. Much of his childhood was
        spent hiking, kayaking and camping throughout the Adirondack Park. An early
        admirer of George Eastman, Jay earned the nickname “The Kodak Kid”, a name
        he still occasionally uses today.
      </p>

      <p>
        Jay earned a Bachelor’s degree in Photography and worked in portrait
        studios, managed photo labs and even went door to door as a portrait
        photographer to support his habit. He soon found opportunities to enter art
        shows and juried exhibitions and have his work printed in various
        publications.
      </p>

      <p>
        Photography also brought Jay some close friendships. While managing a photo
        lab in New York City, he met an older Marine who regularly brought his film
        to Jay to have his pictures developed. The two often met in Central Park to
        take pictures, with cream cheese and jelly sandwiches in tow. Years later,
        Jay was the only one outside of the immediate family who attended the
        funeral.
      </p>

      <p>
        He still has a collection of cameras from different eras, although
        “collection” may be giving it too much credit. Most of them were simply
        cameras he actually used. They represent the evolution of his photography
        from film to digital and mirrorless.
      </p>

      <p>
        After college and the photo-lab years, life became more practical, and
        photography gradually moved into the background. Work, family and other
        responsibilities took priority. It wasn't until someone made an offhand
        comment that Jay should have a better camera than the one he was using that
        he stopped to realize how much he had been missing.
      </p>

      <p>
        Photography had changed a lot and getting back into it felt like catching
        the wave all over again. He joined a rotating artists' group on Long Island,
        displaying photographs in libraries, town offices, tax offices and other
        venues willing to give artists some wall space. Every month brought a new
        location, and Jay's framed prints traveled from building to building in
        large plastic tubs. A few even sold.
      </p>

      <p>
        Jay's professional life eventually took another turn, leading to a Master’s
        degree in Engineering and a career in technology. That technical background
        has never been particularly far from his photography. Cameras, lenses,
        light, exposure, image processing and the technology behind modern
        photography all appeal to the same part of his brain that enjoys
        engineering.
      </p>

      <p>But photography is more than technology.</p>

      <p>
        It is observation. Patience. Light. Timing. Composition. And sometimes
        standing in one place considerably longer than the people you're with think
        is reasonable.
      </p>

      <blockquote class="quote">
        <p>“I want to look at life in the available light.”</p>
        <cite>— Rush, Available Light</cite>
      </blockquote>

      <p>Photography seems to work that way.</p>

      <p>
        There is always another kind of light to understand, another subject to
        photograph, another technique to explore, and another photograph that
        doesn't quite turn out the way you imagined.
      </p>

      <p>That's what Intelligent Imagery is about.</p>

      <p>
        It isn't about having the most expensive camera, knowing every technical
        specification or following a prescribed formula for making photographs. It's
        about learning to see what is actually in front of you, understanding the
        light, making deliberate choices and using the tools available to turn an
        idea into an image.
      </p>

      <p class="callout">Learn to see. Master the light. Capture your world.</p>

      <div class="portrait">
        <Image
          class="portrait-img"
          src={selflakeportrait}
          alt="A self-portrait of Jay Brass by a lake."
          widths={[420, 840]}
          sizes="(max-width: 480px) 92vw, 420px"
        />
      </div>

      <p>
        And, after all these years, Jay is still taking pictures. He also enjoys
        hiking, biking and horseback riding and spending time with his family. In any
        free time he has left, he enjoys writing about himself in the third person.
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .wrapper {
    padding: 1em 1.5em 3em;
  }

  .bio {
    max-width: 720px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    font-size: 1.1em;
    line-height: 1.7;
  }
  .page-title {
    text-align: center;
  }

  /* Prominent lead image. */
  .hero {
    margin: 1rem 0 1.5rem;
    text-align: center;
  }
  .hero-img {
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    border-radius: 8px;
  }

  .lead {
    margin: 0 0 2rem;
    text-align: center;
    font-style: italic;
    font-size: 1.3em;
    line-height: 1.4;
  }

  .bio p {
    margin: 1.1em 0;
  }

  .quote {
    margin: 2.25rem 0;
    padding: 0.25rem 0 0.25rem 1.25rem;
    border-left: 4px solid var(--link);
    font-style: italic;
    font-size: 1.3em;
    line-height: 1.4;
  }
  .quote p {
    margin: 0 0 0.4rem;
  }
  .quote cite {
    font-style: normal;
    font-size: 0.72em;
    color: #5b636b;
  }

  .callout {
    text-align: center;
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: 1.25em;
    color: var(--link);
    margin: 2.25rem 0;
  }

  /* Closing portrait near the bottom — centered, moderate width (it's a tall
     portrait, so it isn't stretched to the full column). */
  .portrait {
    text-align: center;
    margin: 2rem 0;
  }
  .portrait-img {
    width: 100%;
    max-width: 420px;
    height: auto;
    border-radius: 8px;
  }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .wrapper {
      padding: 1em 1em 2.5em;
    }
  }
</style>
